from pathlib import Path
root=Path(__file__).parent
p=root/'proposals/thm-category-o-translation-intertwines-residue-induction-and-restriction.md'
s=p.read_text()
s=s.replace('       def-axiom-of-choice]', '       def-axiom-of-choice,\n       thm-the-whittaker-functor-is-exact-and-fully-faithful-on-the-required-projectives,\n       def-the-whittaker-functor-from-the-relevant-parabolic-o-summand,\n       thm-higher-level-schur-weyl-endomorphism-isomorphism]')
s=s.replace('and the Schur quotient is a module map over the Hecke algebra.', 'and the Schur quotient induces a homomorphism for the enveloping-algebra Chevalley action.')
a=s.index('[F3]'); b=s.index('[F4]',a)
s=s[:a]+'''[F3] The Schur functor is exact, kills exactly the off-standard simples and sends standards to intrinsic Specht modules. Its tensor detector $T_d$ is prinjective, its Hecke endomorphism algebra is $B_d$, and its Whittaker image $U_d$ is a projective generator of the corresponding finite-W image category. There are natural identifications $\gamma_M:\pi_dM\cong\operatorname{Hom}_{W_d}(U_d,\mathrm{Wh}M)$ and the coherent tensor isomorphisms $\nu_M:\mathrm{Wh}(M\otimes V)\cong\mathrm{Wh}(M)\circledast V$, with $\mu_d:U_d\circledast V\cong U_{d+1}$ intertwining the tower generators. In the stable realization $B_d$ is the full cyclotomic algebra, while content projections give the supported blocks. These are the actual Whittaker/Morita and tensor contracts of [[thm-the-whittaker-functor-is-exact-and-fully-faithful-on-the-required-projectives]], [[def-the-whittaker-functor-from-the-relevant-parabolic-o-summand]], [[thm-higher-level-schur-weyl-endomorphism-isomorphism]] and [[thm-schur-functor-images-of-standard-simple-projective-and-tilting-objects]].

'''+s[b:]
a=s.index('## Proof')
s=s[:a]+r'''## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

**Given:** The tensor identity $T_{d+1}=T_d\otimes V$, the actual Whittaker/Morita tensor data of [F3], and the two pairs of residue functors.

1.1 **Restriction uses the dual natural module.** For $N$ in the degree-$(d+1)$ summand and a basis $v_a$ of $V$ with dual basis $v^a$, tensor adjunction gives the natural map $\rho_N:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathfrak g}(T_{d+1},N)\to\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathfrak g}(T_d,N\otimes V^*)$, $\rho_N(h)(t)=\sum_a h(t\otimes v_a)\otimes v^a$. Its inverse sends $g$ to $(t\otimes v)\mapsto(1\otimes\operatorname{ev}_v)g(t)$. These maps are basis-independent by coevaluation and evaluation and commute with the first $d$ tower generators, acting on the $T_d$ factors. Thus they identify restriction of $\pi_{d+1}N$ with $\pi_d(N\otimes V^*)$ after the appropriate degree/block projection. The natural and dual natural modules are distinct $\mathfrak{gl}_n$-modules; no identification between them is used. [F1, F3, construct, algebra]

1.2 **The induction map is an actual tensor map.** For $M$ of degree $d$, put $\tau_M:B_{d+1}\otimes_{B_d}\pi_dM\to\pi_{d+1}(M\otimes V)$, $b\otimes f\mapsto(f\otimes\mathrm{id}_V)\circ R_b$, where $R_b$ is the right action on $T_{d+1}$. The first $d$ generators act on $T_d$, so this expression is balanced over $B_d$ and is $B_{d+1}$-linear for the left-module convention; naturality follows by postcomposition. To prove it invertible, on the finite-W side form $\sigma_X(b\otimes g)=(g\circledast\mathrm{id}_V)\circ\mu_d^{-1}\circ R_b$. For $X=U_d$ this is the regular-module isomorphism $B_{d+1}\otimes_{B_d}B_d\cong B_{d+1}$. It remains an isomorphism on finite sums and summands. Every finite-W image object has a finite projective presentation by summands of finite sums of $U_d$, and both sides are right exact: induction is right exact, tensoring with $V$ is exact and Hom from $U_d,U_{d+1}$ is exact. Passing to cokernels proves $\sigma_X$ invertible for all $X$. The coherent $\gamma,\nu,\mu$ square of [F3] sends $1\otimes f$ by either route to $(\mathrm{Wh}(f)\circledast\mathrm{id}_V)\mu_d^{-1}$; $B_{d+1}$-linearity then makes the square commute on every $b\otimes f$. Consequently $\tau_M$ is invertible for every $M$. This presentation argument takes place in the finite-W image category, where $U_d$ is a full projective generator; it does not assert that $T_d$ is a full category-O generator. [F3, construct, algebra]

2.1 **The two residue projectors.** The tower's last dot on $T_d\otimes V$ is the tensor Casimir, with the normalization of [F1]. Hence $\tau$ intertwines its generalized-$i$ eigenprojector with the projector defining $f_i^{\mathcal O}$. On the dual tensor factor the adjunction mate is the dual Casimir: the flag calculation of [F1] gives eigenvalue $-n-i$ for the move $i+1\to i$, not $i$. Under $\rho$ the last-dot projector therefore corresponds to the generalized-$(-n-i)$ projector defining $e_i^{\mathcal O}$. Applying the source content projector $e_\alpha$ and target projector $e_{\alpha+\alpha_i}$ to steps 1.1–1.2 gives $u_{\alpha,i}\pi_{\alpha+\alpha_i}N\cong\pi_\alpha e_i^{\mathcal O}N$ and $B_{\alpha+\alpha_i}u_{\alpha,i}\otimes_{B_\alpha}\pi_\alpha M\cong\pi_{\alpha+\alpha_i}f_i^{\mathcal O}M$. Polynomial generalized-eigenspace projectors commute with these natural maps because the maps intertwine the operators, proving the asserted residue compatibility. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]

2.2 **Adjunction maps.** The first tensor unit is the coevaluation $m\mapsto\sum_a(m\otimes v_a)\otimes v^a$, and the tensor counit is evaluation. The induction/restriction unit is $z\mapsto1\otimes z$, and the counit is $b\otimes z\mapsto bz$. Substituting these formulas into $\tau$ and $\rho$ gives the same composite maps, term by term: coevaluation is precisely the sum in $\rho$, and evaluation cancels that sum by $v^a(v_b)=\delta_{ab}$. Restricting the commuting squares by the content and residue projectors proves compatibility for $f_i\dashv e_i$. For the reverse adjunction use the biadjoint mate maps supplied by [F1] and the symmetric-trace reverse adjunction of [F2]; transport the latter through $\tau,\rho$. The transported evaluation and coevaluation pair is the O-side biadjoint pair, since its dot and crossing mates agree with those in [F1], with the same trace normalization. Equivalently the reverse unit and counit are the dual mates of the first pair, so the same squares commute. The two pairs are thus compared with their transported normalizations; this is stronger than asserting abstract uniqueness of adjoints across a quotient. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, construct, algebra]

3.1 **Duality is contravariant, not tensor dualization.** The ordinary category-O duality uses matrix transpose and restricted weight-space duals. The product of the ground contravariant form and the natural-factor forms gives the detector form $(xtb,t')=(t,x^{\mathrm{tr}}t'b^*)$, so its endomorphism anti-involution is the Hecke $*$. The natural duality comparison $\pi D\cong D_H\pi$ is the actual prinjective/Hom comparison of [F3]; it is not the usual tensor identity $V\cong V^*$. The residue Casimir and the content projectors are self-adjoint for these forms, so this comparison commutes with each of $f_i$ and $e_i$ separately, as do the dualities in [F1]–[F2]. It also identifies their dual adjunction maps used in step 2.2. In particular $Df_i\cong f_iD$ and $De_i\cong e_iD$, rather than exchanging the two residue functors. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]

3.2 **Grothendieck operators and quotient.** Exactness of $\pi$ and of the residue functors makes the natural isomorphisms of step 2.1 induce $[f_i^H][\pi]=[\pi][f_i^{\mathcal O}]$ and $[e_i^H][\pi]=[\pi][e_i^{\mathcal O}]$. On $G_0$, the source simple-class basis shows that $\ker[\pi]$ is exactly the span of the off-standard simple classes, and the induced map from this quotient onto $G_0(H)$ is an isomorphism. No isomorphism from the entire category-O Grothendieck group is asserted. On split projective groups only $\operatorname{add}(T)$ is equivalent to Hecke projectives; an ambient off-standard projective need not have projective image. These distinctions leave the displayed operator equalities intact. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 **Filtrations and boundaries.** Applying exact $\pi$ to the O-side residue standard flags preserves their successive quotients, and the intertwined generalized-eigenvalue projectors select the corresponding residue pieces. Admissible nonintegral origins retain the typed coset realization of [F4]; an integral highest-weight Fock interpretation is used only on its actual integral residue component. All comparisons here are ungraded at parameter one. Together with steps 2.1–3.2 this proves the statement. [F1, F3, F4, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1, step 3.2, given] ∎

## Remarks

The raising tensor factor is $V$ and the lowering tensor factor is $V^*$. Their adjunction is evaluation/coevaluation. Restricted transpose duality fixes the natural factor and commutes with each residue functor; these are different constructions.
'''
p.write_text(s)
